<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">French Upper Volta</h1><p class="st-meta">1919 CE – 1958 CE · capital Ouagadougou</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The French colony centred on the Mossi kingdoms, abolished in 1932 and recreated in 1947, which became independent in 1960 and is now Burkina Faso.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had conquered the region in the 1890s, taking <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ouagadougou">Ouagadougou</a>, capital of the Kingdom of Ouagadougou, in 1896. The lands were first ruled as part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-sudan">French Sudan</a>, and in 1919 the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-volta">Upper Volta</a> was created, with its capital at Ouagadougou and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edouard-hesling">Edouard Hesling</a> as its first governor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony, part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa</a>, was densely populated but poor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ruled through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> kings, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mogho-naba">Mogho Naba</a>, and used the colony as a reservoir of labour for railways and for plantations in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-ivory-coast">French Ivory Coast</a>. In 1932 it was abolished and divided among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-sudan">French Sudan</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-niger">Colony of Niger</a>, partly to supply labour.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> chiefs and politicians lobbied for its restoration, achieved in 1947. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-volta">Upper Volta</a> became an autonomous republic in 1958 and independent on 5 August 1960 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-yameogo">Maurice Yameogo</a>. It was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a> in 1984.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-upper-volta.jpg" alt="French Upper Volta" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1919 CE – 1958 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ouagadougou</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Edouard Hesling, Maurice Yameogo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion, Sunni Islam, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Moore, Dyula</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Upper Volta (colony), Haute-Volta</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1938&amp;focus=french-upper-volta" aria-label="French Upper Volta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4638 1717 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French Upper Volta lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4958 1973L5054 728M4958 1973L4523 1888M4958 1973L4781 1962"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M4523 1888h0M4781 1962h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4958 1973h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4958 1973h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ouagadougou. <a href="/atlas?year=1938&amp;focus=french-upper-volta">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
